Here's the full audit of `SerializerTests.cs`.

---

## Summary: 4 Critical · 0 High · 2 Medium · 0 Low

The suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions** — tests that pick inputs already in the output's fixed-point so `output == input` is trivially true, or that assert a field against itself through a pass-through method. One test asserts a lossless round-trip on a **lossy mapper**, which means its assertions are either dead or wrong.

---

## Critical Findings

### 1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Tautological input

```csharp
var input = "HELLO";            // already uppercase
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result); // "HELLO" == "HELLO" — always true
```

`ToUpper()` on an already-uppercase string is a no-op. This test passes even if `ToUpper` were replaced with an identity function.

**Fix:** use an input that actually requires transformation and assert against an independent literal:

```csharp
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

### 2. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Tautological input

```csharp
var name = "alice";                          // already trimmed + lowercase
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);              // trivially true
```

Production code does `name.Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. Feeding in `"alice"` means no trimming and no case change occurs — the test never exercises the actual transformation.

**Fix:** test with input that *needs* normalizing, and also keep the already-normalized case as a separate boundary test:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void NormalizeName_MixedCaseWithWhitespace_NormalizesToLower()
{
    var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
    Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);        // literal expected value
}

[TestMethod]
public void NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_ReturnsSameValue()
{
    var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("alice");
    Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);        // fine as a boundary test
}
```

---

### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion

```csharp
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);  // asserts dto.Name == dto.Name
```

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` (line 10 of production code). The test asserts the output equals the same property the method reads from — it is structurally `x == x`. If `GetDisplayName` were later changed to return `$"{Name} (Age: {Age})"`, this test would correctly catch that, but it would also pass for *any* implementation that returns `Name`, including a broken one.

**Fix:** assert against an independent literal:

```csharp
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
```

---

### 4. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45–53) — Tautological round-trip on a lossy mapper

```csharp
var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);        // Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 0  (Id, Email lost)
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto); // Id = 0, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = ""
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);           // 1 vs 0 — FAILS
Assert.AreEqual(entity.FullName, backToEntity.FullName); // only one that can pass
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);     // "a@b.com" vs "" — FAILS
```

Production code shows `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `ToEntity` hardcodes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. Two of the three assertions *cannot* pass. If the suite is reported green, these tests are not actually running.

**Fix:** test each mapping direction independently against literal expected values:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void MapToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);  // documents that Age is not mapped
}

[TestMethod]
public void MapToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);    // documents that Id is not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // documents that Email is not mapped
}
```

---

## Medium Findings

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Detail | Fix |
